Practical Lab

Step1: As I have cloned my ubuntu VM, so I have reduce VM resources as per my local host specification 

1. First, reduce your VM resources

Before starting the lab, shut down your existing Ubuntu VM.

In VMware:

VM → Settings → Memory

For the iSCSI Server:

Memory: 1536 MB
Processors: 1

For the iSCSI Client:

Memory: 1024–1536 MB
Processors: 1

If Ubuntu Server is being used, 1 GB can be enough for this lab.

If you're using Ubuntu Desktop, I'd prefer:

Server VM: 1.5 GB
Client VM: 1.5 GB

Also close unnecessary Windows applications while both VMs are running.

Step2: Don't create two new VMs, I am using only 1 vm as my local host specification is low

Add a second virtual disk

Since everything is inside one VM, create an additional virtual disk in VMware.

VMware
Power off Ubuntu.
Open VMware Workstation.
Select your Ubuntu VM.
Go to VM Settings.
Click Add.
Select Hard Disk.
Select SCSI.
Create a new virtual disk.
Give it something like:
5 GB
Finish.
Start Ubuntu.

Now check:

lsblk

You should see a new disk, for example:

sda     30G
sdb      5G

Your new disk may be /dev/sdb.

Do not blindly assume /dev/sdb; always confirm with lsblk

Step 3: Install iSCSI target software

Ubuntu uses LIO as the kernel target framework, commonly managed through targetcli.

Install it:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install targetcli-fb -y

Check:

targetcli

You should enter something similar to:

/> 

Exit:

/> exit

Step4: Identify your new disk

Run:

lsblk

Suppose your output shows:

NAME   SIZE
sda     30G
sdb      5G

We'll use:

/dev/sdb

as the iSCSI storage.

VERY IMPORTANT

Before continuing, make sure /dev/sdb is really the new empty disk.

You can check:

sudo fdisk -l

Step5: Create the iSCSI Target

Start:

sudo targetcli

You will see:

/>

First create a backstore using your new disk:

/backstores/block create iscsi_disk /dev/sdb

Check:

ls

You should see:

backstores

Then:

/backstores/block ls

You should see something like:

o- block ........................................ [Storage Objects: 1]
  o- iscsi_disk ........ [/dev/sdb]

Step6: Create an iSCSI target

Inside targetcli:

/iscsi create iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage

You have now created an iSCSI target.

Think of it like:

Target
   |
   └── "Here is the storage I am offering"

Check:

/iscsi ls

You should see:

iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage

Step7: Create a LUN

Now connect your storage disk to the iSCSI target.

Run:

/iscsi/iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage/tpg1/luns create /backstores/block/iscsi_disk

Check:

/iscsi/iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage/tpg1/luns ls

You should see:

lun0

So your structure is now:

/dev/sdb
   ↓
Backstore
   ↓
LUN 0
   ↓
iSCSI Target

Step8: Enable the iSCSI target

Inside targetcli:

/iscsi/iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage/tpg1 set attribute authentication=0

For this lab only, we'll keep authentication simple.

Now check the portal:

/iscsi/iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage/tpg1/portals ls

You should normally have:

0.0.0.0:3260

Port 3260 is the standard iSCSI TCP port.

Exit:

exit

Save the configuration:

sudo targetctl save

Check the service:

sudo systemctl status rtslib-fb-targetctl

Step9: Install the iSCSI Initiator

Now comes an important concept.

The same Ubuntu VM will act as the iSCSI client/initiator.

Install the initiator:

sudo apt install open-iscsi -y

Enable it:

sudo systemctl enable --now iscsid

Check:

sudo systemctl status iscsid

Step9: Find your iSCSI Initiator IQN

Run:

cat /etc/iscsi/initiatorname.iscsi

You'll see something similar to:

InitiatorName=iqn.1993-08.org.debian:01.xxxxxxxxx

This is the identity of your iSCSI client.

Think:

Target
   = Storage server

Initiator
   = Storage client

In our single-VM lab:

Ubuntu VM
 ├── Target
 └── Initiator

Step10: Discover the iSCSI Target

So far, you created the iSCSI Target on your Ubuntu VM.

Think of it like this:

Your Ubuntu VM
│
├── iSCSI Target
│      │
│      └── Storage/LUN
│
└── iSCSI Initiator
       ↑
       We now need to find the Target

The Initiator needs to ask:

Are there any iSCSI storage targets available at this IP address?

==> In my case the Target and Initiator are on the same VM, we can use:

127.0.0.1

This means:

“This computer itself.”

You don't need to find another server's IP for this single-VM lab.

==> Run the discovery command

Run:

sudo iscsiadm -m discovery -t sendtargets -p 127.0.0.1

Let's break it down:

sudo
 ↓
Run with administrator privileges

iscsiadm
 ↓
iSCSI administration command

-m discovery
 ↓
Discovery mode

-t sendtargets
 ↓
Ask the iSCSI server to send me its available targets

-p 127.0.0.1
 ↓
Look for the target on this machine

You should get something similar to:

127.0.0.1:3260,1 iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage
What does this mean?
127.0.0.1
     ↓
Your Ubuntu VM

3260
     ↓
iSCSI port

iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage
     ↓
Your iSCSI Target name

So the result basically says:

“I found an iSCSI target at 127.0.0.1, using port 3260, and its name is iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage.”

Step11: Login to the iSCSI Target

Now we move from discovery → login.

Discovery only means:

"I found the storage."

Login means:

"I want to connect to and use this storage."

Run:

sudo iscsiadm -m node --login

You should get something similar to:

Logging in to [iface: default, target: iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage, portal: 127.0.0.1,3260]
Login to [iface: default, target: iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage, portal: 127.0.0.1,3260] successful.
Then check the iSCSI session

Run:

sudo iscsiadm -m session

You should see something like:

tcp: [1] 127.0.0.1:3260,1 iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage

This means:

iSCSI Initiator
       │
       │ LOGIN
       ↓
127.0.0.1:3260
       │
       ↓
iSCSI Target
       │
       ↓
LUN / Storage

**************

Note:- 

==> In my case, Initially, discovery worked:

127.0.0.1:3260,1 iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage

This proved:

Initiator ────────> Target
         Discovery
             ✅

But when you tried:

sudo iscsiadm -m node --login

you received:

iSCSI login failed due to authorization failure

==> Why did it happen?

The iSCSI target knew about the initiator, but the initiator was not authorized through the Target Portal Group ACL.

Your initiator had this IQN:

iqn.2004-10.com.ubuntu:01:b071b9e1d734

But that IQN wasn't present in the target's ACL.

Think of an ACL like an access list:

Target
  |
  └── ACL
       |
       ├── Initiator A ✅
       ├── Initiator B ✅
       └── My Initiator ❌

So:

Discovery
   ↓
Target found ✅
   ↓
Login
   ↓
Authorization check
   ↓
Initiator not in ACL ❌
   ↓
Login rejected

==> How we fixed it

We created an ACL for your Initiator IQN:

/iscsi/iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage/tpg1/acls create iqn.2004-10.com.ubuntu:01:b071b9e1d734

This effectively told the target:

"Allow this initiator to access my iSCSI target."

Then we checked the authentication configuration and disabled authentication for this lab environment:

/iscsi/iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage/tpg1 set attribute authentication=0

After that:

sudo iscsiadm -m node --login

worked successfully.

************

Now we have successfully completed:
1. Create Target       ✅
2. Create LUN          ✅
3. Configure ACL       ✅
4. Discover Target     ✅
5. Login               ✅
6. Active Session      ✅

Now we need to check whether the iSCSI LUN has appeared as a disk.

Step12: Identify the iSCSI Disk

Run:

lsblk

Don't format anything yet.

Also run:

sudo fdisk -l

And run, you will get

ls -l /dev/disk/by-path/ | grep iscsi

Output:

ip-127.0.0.1:3260-iscsi-iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage-lun-0 -> ../../sdd

*******
==> What does this mean?

The important part is:

-> ../../sdd

So your iSCSI LUN is:

/dev/sdd

********

And we can see the complete relationship:

127.0.0.1:3260
       ↓
iSCSI Target
iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage
       ↓
LUN 0
       ↓
/dev/sdd

Step14: It's a safety/verification step. It is very important because mkfs can erase a disk.

We discovered this:

iSCSI LUN 0 -> /dev/sdd

So we think /dev/sdd is our iSCSI disk.

But before doing:

sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdd

we need to be 100% sure that /dev/sdd is the correct disk.

Why?

Because:

mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdd

means:

Create a new filesystem on /dev/sdd. Existing data on that disk can be destroyed.

So we don't want to accidentally format your Ubuntu OS disk.

*********

Imagine you have three disks:

/dev/sda → Ubuntu operating system
/dev/sdb → Some other data
/dev/sdd → iSCSI LUN

We want:

/dev/sdd
   ↓
Format
   ↓
ext4

But if we accidentally run:

sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda

we could damage the Ubuntu installation.

Therefore:

Part 13
   ↓
Find the iSCSI disk
   ↓
/dev/sdd
   ↓
Part 14
   ↓
VERIFY /dev/sdd
   ↓
Part 15
   ↓
Format /dev/sdd

********

Step15: Create filesystem on the iSCSI LUN

Once confirmed, run:

sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdd

You should get output similar to:

Creating filesystem with ...
Filesystem UUID: xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx
What just happened?

We took:

/dev/sdd

which is a raw block device, and created an ext4 filesystem on it.

Think:

Raw LUN
   ↓
/dev/sdd
   ↓
mkfs.ext4
   ↓
ext4 filesystem
   ↓
Ready to mount

Step16: Create mount point

Create a directory:

sudo mkdir /mnt/iscsi

Mount the iSCSI LUN:

sudo mount /dev/sdd /mnt/iscsi

Check:

df -h /mnt/iscsi

You should see something similar to:

Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on
/dev/sdd        4.8G   ...  ...   ... /mnt/iscsi

At this point, your iSCSI storage is actually usable.

*******

The complete flow you've now built

This is worth remembering for your interview:

VMware Virtual Disk
       ↓
   iSCSI Target
       ↓
     LUN 0
       ↓
 iSCSI Initiator
       ↓
    /dev/sdd
       ↓
   ext4 filesystem
       ↓
   /mnt/iscsi
       ↓
      Files

*********

Step17: Test read/write

We should do a read/write test:

echo "iSCSI lab test" | sudo tee /mnt/iscsi/test.txt

Then:

cat /mnt/iscsi/test.txt

Expected:

iSCSI lab test

Step18: Check the iSCSI session

Run:

sudo iscsiadm -m session

You should see:

tcp: [2] 127.0.0.1:3260,1 iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage (non-flash)

This confirms that the iSCSI connection is still active.

Step19: Unmount the iSCSI filesystem

Now we'll test disconnecting the storage.

First, make sure you're not currently inside /mnt/iscsi:

cd ~

Then:

sudo umount /mnt/iscsi

Verify:

df -h | grep iscsi

You should get no output.

==> You can also check:

mount | grep iscsi

Again, no output means it is unmounted.

*********

Why are we doing this?

We're testing:

Mounted
   ↓
Use storage
   ↓
Unmount

*******

Step20: Logout from the iSCSI target

Now disconnect the iSCSI session:

sudo iscsiadm -m node --logout

Expected:

Logging out of session [sid: 2, target: iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage, portal: 127.0.0.1,3260]
Logout of [sid: 2, target: iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage, portal: 127.0.0.1,3260] successful.

Check:

sudo iscsiadm -m session

You should get something like:

iscsiadm: No active sessions.

This means:

iSCSI session
     ↓
   Logged out

Step21: Check the disk after logout

Run:

lsblk

The iSCSI disk /dev/sdd may no longer appear as an active iSCSI device.

==> Why is this useful?

It demonstrates that the block device was provided through the iSCSI session.

Step22: Login again

Now let's reconnect.

Run:

sudo iscsiadm -m node --login

Expected:

Login to [iface: default, target: iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage, portal: 127.0.0.1,3260] successful.

Check:

sudo iscsiadm -m session

You should again see:

tcp: [2] 127.0.0.1:3260,1 iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage

Step22: Verify the LUN again

Run:

lsblk

Then:

ls -l /dev/disk/by-path/ | grep iscsi

You should again see:

...iscsi-iqn.2026-08.local.lab:storage-lun-0 -> ../../sdd

So:

Logout
   ↓
LUN disappears
   ↓
Login
   ↓
LUN appears again

Step23: Mount the iSCSI LUN again

Mount it:

sudo mount /dev/sdd /mnt/iscsi

Check:

df -h /mnt/iscsi

Step24: Verify the old data

This is the important test.

Run:

cat /mnt/iscsi/test.txt

You should still get:

This data is stored on iSCSI LUN

This proves that the data survived:

Write data
    ↓
Unmount
    ↓
Logout
    ↓
Login again
    ↓
Mount
    ↓
Data still exists

*********

